      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 94,
      "outputTokens": 762,
      "costUsd": 0.0069050000000000005,
      "pronunciationText": "So far, every random variable counted something. Now X can be any value in an interval, because we measure it. At an athletics carnival, sensors in the starting blocks time each sprinter's reaction to the start signal. One reads zero point three five seconds. But a finer timer shows zero point three five one seven, with more digits after that. So what's the probability that a reaction time is exactly zero point three five seconds? Make a guess."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-02",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-s04/scene-02.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1074,
      "worstWordGap": {
        "seconds": 0.9220000000001356,
        "after": "ones.",
        "before": "Instead,"
      },
      "cues": [
        {
          "text": "twenty",
          "start": 30.205000000000105,
          "end": 30.480142857142962
        }
      ],
      "seconds": 35.8,
      "peakFraction": 0.838592529296875,
      "fullScaleSamples": 0,
      "silencesAtLeast1_5s": [],
      "lowScoreWords": [],
      "successfulRequestCount": 1,
      "loggedRequestAttempts": 1,
      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 114,
      "outputTokens": 895,
      "costUsd": 0.008112,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's an example set of two hundred reaction times, from the carnival's heats. Time is a continuous random variable, which here means it can take any value in an interval. For an ideal continuous measurement, exact repeats have probability zero, so counting each value gives just ones. Instead, we group the times into classes, each a tenth of a second wide. Twenty land between zero point one and zero point two seconds, then fifty, seventy, forty and twenty. That's a histogram. Its bars touch, because the classes leave no gaps."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-03",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-s04/scene-03.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 704,
      "worstWordGap": {
        "seconds": 0.8419999999999987,
        "after": "six.",
        "before": "Each"
      },
      "cues": [
        {
          "text": "six",
          "start": 14.541999999999991,
          "end": 14.67699999999999
        }
      ],
      "seconds": 23.44,
      "peakFraction": 0.84466552734375,
      "fullScaleSamples": 0,
      "silencesAtLeast1_5s": [],
      "lowScoreWords": [
        {
          "word": "six.",
          "start": 14.541999999999991,
          "end": 14.72199999999999,
          "score": 0.436
        }
      ],
      "successfulRequestCount": 1,
      "loggedRequestAttempts": 1,
      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 83,
      "outputTokens": 586,
      "costUsd": 0.0053155,
      "pronunciationText": "What's the probability that a time is at least zero point two, but less than zero point four seconds? Two classes cover that range, with fifty and seventy times. So the estimate is one hundred and twenty out of two hundred, which is zero point six. Each class's relative frequency is its count over two hundred, and they add to one, because every time lands in exactly one class."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-04",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-s04/scene-04.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 738,
