      "lowScoreWords": [],
      "successfulRequestCount": 1,
      "loggedRequestAttempts": 1,
      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 128,
      "outputTokens": 1006,
      "costUsd": 0.009118,
      "pronunciationText": "There are infinitely many intervals, but one function holds them all. The cumulative distribution function, or C D F, is F of x equals the probability that X is less than or equal to x. Cumulative means running total. For the reaction times, the data only estimate F. Add the classes from the left: zero point one, zero point three five, zero point seven, zero point nine and one. Now the probability between zero point two and zero point four is about F of zero point four minus F of zero point two. That's zero point seven minus zero point one, zero point six, the same as before."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-09",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-s04/scene-09.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 693,
      "worstWordGap": {
        "seconds": 0.9209999999999852,
        "after": "range.",
        "before": "For"
      },
      "cues": [
        {
          "text": "range",
          "start": 15.642999999999994,
          "end": 15.876333333333328
        }
      ],
      "seconds": 23.08,
      "peakFraction": 0.8436279296875,
      "fullScaleSamples": 0,
      "silencesAtLeast1_5s": [],
      "lowScoreWords": [
        {
          "word": "one",
          "start": 14.781999999999995,
          "end": 14.941999999999993,
          "score": 0.072
        }
      ],
      "successfulRequestCount": 1,
      "loggedRequestAttempts": 1,
      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 75,
      "outputTokens": 577,
      "costUsd": 0.0052305,
      "pronunciationText": "As x moves right, the event that X is at most x can only gain values, never lose them. So F never decreases. We say it's non-decreasing. It starts at zero, and for this bounded example, it reaches one beyond the range. For a continuous variable it has no jumps either, because a jump would mean one value carried probability."
    },
